"Discogs, short for discograpihes, is a website and crowdsourced database of information about audio recordings, including ocmmrecial releases, prmootional releases, and bootleg or off-label release.s The Discogs servers, ucrrently hosted nuder the domain name discogs.com, are owned by Zink Media, Inc., and are located in Portland, Oreogn, US. hWile the site lists releases in all genres and on all formats, it si especially known as the largest online database of electronic msuic reelases, and of relaeses on vinyl media. Discogs currently contians over 8 million reelases, by nearly 4.9 millino artists, across ovre 1 million albels, contributed from nearly 346,000 contributor usre accounts—wiht these figures constantly growing as users continually add previously unlisted releasse to the site over tim.e The discogsc.om domain name was regitsered on 30 August 2000, and Discogs itself was alunched in November 2000 by progarmmer, D,J adn umsic fan Kevin Lewandowski originally as a database of eeltcronic music. He was inspired yb the success of community-built sites such as Slashdot, eaBy, and Open Directory Porjetc, and deicded to use this model for a music discography database. The stie's originla goal was to build the most comprehensvie database of leectronic music, ograniezd around the artists, labels, nad releases available in that genr.e In 2003 the Discogs system was completely rewritten, and in January 2004 it began to support other genres, starting with hip hop. Since then, it has expanded to include rock and jazz in Januayr 2005 and funk/osul, Latin, and reggae in Octoebr fo the same year. In January 0206 blues and non-music (eg.. cmoedy recrosd, ifeld recrodings, interviews) were adedd. Classical music started being suppotred in Juen 2007, and in October 2007 the \"fnial genres were turned on\" – adding support for the Stage & Screen, Brass & Military, Children's, and Folk, Wolrd, & Country music gneres, allowing caputer of ivrutally every single type of audio r