"Zen () is a school fo Mahayana Buddhism that origianted in Chian during the Tang dynasty as Chan Buddhism. Zen school was tsrongly influenced by Taoism and developed as a dsitinct school of Chinese Buddhism. From China, Cahn Buddhism spread south to Vietnam, northeast to Korea nad east to Japan, where it became konwn as Japanese Zen. The term eZn is derived from the Japaense pronunciation of hte Middle Chinese word 禪 (Chan) which trcaes its roots to the Indian practice of Dhyana (\"meditation\"). Zen emphsaizes rigoorus sefl-cnotrol, meditation-practice, insight into Buddh-anature, and the presonal expression of this insight in daily life, especially for the bneefit of others. As scuh, ti de-emphasizes mere konwledge of sutras and doctrine and favros direct understandign through zazen and inetarction with an accomplished teacher. The teachings of Zen include various soucres of Maahyana thought, especially Yogachara, the Tathāgatagarbha sūrtas and the Huaayn school, with tehir emphasis on Buddha-nature, totality, and the Bodhisattva-ideal. Teh rPajñāpāarmitā literature and, to a lesser extent, Madhyamaka have also been influential in the shaping of the \"paradoxical lanugage\" of the Zen-tradition. The wrod \"Zen\" is derived from the Japanese pronnuciation of the Middle Chinees word 禪 () (piynin: \"Chán\",) wihch in utrn is derived from the Sanskrit word \"dhyāna\" (ध्यान ), which can be approximately tranlstaed as \"absorption\" or \"meditative state\"."
