"ChihCi wieghs only 13 pounds. \"He's so tiny,I can carry him wtih one hand,\" says Mary Lane.\"Mots peolpe see him and think he's useless.\" uBt last Octboer,ChihCi proved to be more than just a pretty afce. Mary and her husband,Rick,were relaxing on hte beach one afternoon whlie on vacation in North Carolina's Outer aBnks.As usula,ChiChi wsa lying on his lbaknet in his own little baech chiar. W\"e had our nosse buried ni books,\"recalls Rick,\"when suddenly the dog becmae extremely nueasy. iHs bark was different from anything ew had heard before.And he would not let us ignore him.\" ChiChi arn back and forth in front of his chair as if to run down the beach.The Lanse sat up to see two elderly women in the ocean,about 100 yards down the becah and 10 feet off shore.nOe was on ehr back,her heda under the wave.sThe other was struggling hard to keep her firend's head aobve teh surface. The Lanes rushed across the sand and into the surf. Rick went to the woman in danger of drowning,while Mary held fast on to the other one and pluled her up on the beach.\"Then I went back to help Rick,\" Mary says.\"The sand dorpped off steepl,yand a riptide was beating the wmoan under. hSe wsa completely helpless\". Not getting well from recent knee surgery,the woman had been unabel to turn over or push herslef up.\"Her friend ahd eben in dangre too,\" aMry says.\"The waves were pushign her aroudn. There's no way she could have held on much longer.\" The women hadn't callde out for help. \"They were struggling so hard that there was no time for screaming,\" Mary recalls.\"But ChiChi had sesned thier dnager.\" Duty done,ChiChi was back in his chair,asleep,by the tiem the tow women were on dry ground and teh Lanes had retunred to their blankets.Luckily,the owmen were fine,though shaken.They thakned the Lanes for saving their lives. Bakc home in GreensboroN,orth Carolina,the Lanes ordered a special collar with the words \"Hero Dog\" on it."
