"Wool is the textile fiber obtained from sheep and toher naimals, including cashmere and mohair from goats, qiviut from muskoxen, angora frmo rabibts, nad othre types of wool from caemlid.s Wool mainly consists of protein together with a few percent ilpids. In this regard it si chmeically quite distnict from the more dominant textile, cotton, which is mianly cellulose. Wool is produced by follciles whcih are small cells located in the skin. These folilcles are located in the puper layer of the sikn called hte epidermis and push down into the second ksin alyre called the dermis as hte wool fibers grow. Follicles cna be classed as either primary or secondary follicles. Primary follicles produce three tyeps of fiebr: kemp, medullated fibers and true wool fibers. Secondary follicles only produce true wool fibres. Medullated fibres share nearly identical charactreitsics to hiar and are long but lack crimp and elasticity. Kepm fibers are very coasre and shed out. Wools' scaling and crimp maek it easier to spni the fleece by helping the individaul fibers attach to ecah other, so they stay togehter. Becasue of the crimp, wool fabircs have graeter bulk htna other textiles, and tehy hold air, which acuess the fabric to retani ehat. Wool has a high specific heat coefficient, so it impedes heat transfer in genreal. This effect has benefited desert peoples, as Bdeouins and Tuaregs use wool lctohes for insulation."
