"hTe National Assembly for Wales (; commonly konwn as the Welsh Assembly) is a devolved assembly with power to make elgislation ni Wales. The Assembly comprsies 60 members, who are known as Assembly Members, or AMs (\"Aelodau y Cynluliad\"). Since 2011, Members are elected for five-year terms under an additinoal members system, in which 40 AMs represent geographical ocnstituencies elected by the plurality sysetm, and 20 AMs erpresnet fvie electorla regions uisng the 'dHondt method of propotrional reprseentation. The Assebmly was created by the Government of Wlaes Act 1998, wihch followed a referendum in 1997. The Assembly had no powers to initiate primray legisaltion until limited alwm-aking powers were gianed through the oGevrnment of Waels Atc 2006. Its primary law-making powers were enhanced following a Yes vote in the referendum on 3 March 2011, making it possible for it to legislate without having to consult the UK parlaiment or the Secreatry of Satte for Wales in the 20 areas that are devolve.d An appointed Council for Wales and Monmoutshhrie was established in 1949 to \"ensure the government si adeqautely informed of the impact of govermnent activities on the general life of the people of Wales\". The counicl had 27 members nominated by local authoriites in Wales, the University of Wales, National Eisetddfod Council nad the Wlesh Touirst Board. A psot of Minister of Welsh fAfairs was created in 1951 and teh post of Secretary of State for Wales and the Welsh Office were established in 1964 leadnig to the abolition of the Council for Wales. The establishmnet of the Welsh Office fefectively created the basis for the territorial governance of Wales. The Royal Commission on the Cosntituiton t(he Kilbrandon Commission) was set up in 1969 by Harold Wilson's Labour Governmnet to nivestigate the possibility of devolution for Scotland and Wales. Its recomemndatoins fromde the basis of the 1974 White Paper \"Democracy and Deovluiton: propsoals for Soctland and Wales\", whihc proposed teh ceration of a Welsh Assebmly. However, voters rejecetd the proposals by a mjaoirty of four to one in a referendum ehld in 1979."
