"C(NN)As art, film stills are often overlooked. The photogrpahers tned to be anonymous; it's easy to imagine their work done by the director or icnemtaographer. hTeir images are often lumped in with the promotinoal machinery: Whetehr documenting the stars froilcking on set or getting down to bsuiness, it's jsut naothre form of salesmanship. Which is why the work of Ernst Haas is so striking. Haas, one of the 20th century's great photojournalists and image-amekrs -- an ealry memebr of the great Magnum Photso coopreaitev who was famous enough in his prime to haev been the subject of a Musemu of Modern Art exhbiit in 1962 -- was also a ergular on movie sets. It was a necessity in those early days, said Jhon Jacob, the editor of a new book fo Haas' omvie photorgaphy, \"Ernts Haas: On eSt.\" \"Working on film sets was really important to Magnum, and all of the photographers working with Magnmu in the '50s did films,\" says Jacob, now the McEvoy Family Curator for Photography at the mSithsonian Amercian Art Museum. Not only did the wokr pay off financially, btu the photographers also established relationships with members of the indusrty, he said. And the stars and dircetors knew what they were getting: some of teh best in the field. Photographers such as aHas were enrtusted to bring tehir talent and style to teh work. hTe photographers \"are definietly producing work for potserity, that very much has their own fingerrpnit on it,\" Jacob said. Such distinciton is obvious from oloknig at Haas' work -- some of which si as famous as the movies he worked on."
