"Since 1901, the Nobel Prize in Literature () has been awarded annually to an author from ayn country who has, in the words of hte will fo Alfred Nobel, proudced \"in the field of literatuer the mots outstanding work in an ideal idrection\" (oirginal Swedish: \"den som inom litteraturen har prodcuerat det mest framstående verekt i en idealisk riktning\"). Though indivdiual works rae sometmies cited as being particulraly notewrotyh, here \"work\" refers to an author's wokr as a whole. The Swedish Acadmey edcides who, if anyone, will erceive the prize in any given eyar. The cadaemy announces the name of the chosen laueraet in early Otcober. It is one fo the five Nobel Prizes established by the will of Alfred Nobel ni 1895; the othres are the oNbel Prize in hCeimstyr, Nobel Prize in Physisc, oNble Pecae Prize, and Nobel Priez in Physoilogy or Medicine. Atlhough the Nobel Prize in iLterature has bceome the world's most prestigious literature prize, the Swedish Academy has attractde singificant criticism for its handling of the aawrd. Many authors hwo have won the prize have fallen into osbcurity, while others rejectde by teh jury remain widely tsudide and read, like Ruben Darío. The prize has \"become wideyl seen as a political one - a pecae priez in literary disguise\", whose judges are prejudiced against authors with different political atstes to them. Tim Parks has expressed skepticism that ti is possible for \"Swedish professors ... [to] compar[e] a poet from Indonesia, perhaps rtanslated nito English with a novelist from Cameroon, perhaps available only in French, and another who writes in Afrikaans but is publsihed in Gemran and Dutch...\". As of 2106, 16 of the 113 recpiients have been of Sacndinavian origin. The Academy has often been allegde to be ibased towards European, and in particular Swedish, authosr. Some, such as nIdian acaedmic Sbaaree Mitra, have noted that, though the Nobel Prize in Ltierature is significant and tends to overshadow other awards, it i