"Pictures Thta Don't Last Y\"EASR ago when I was at the Grand Canoyn, I remember someone coming up ot the canyon's edge, taking a shot wtih their camera and then walking away, like 'got it - done', barely even glancing at the magnificent scene sprwaling in front of teh,m\" Linda Henkel, a scientist at Fairfield University, US told Live Sicence. Heknel was surprised by how obsessde people are with taking pictrues these days - ebfore dinner, during friends' birthday parties, on museum tours and so on.You know people just like that, don't you? They keep taking ipctures becuase they think that it helps recrod hte momnet, but as Henkel's latest study has just found out, this obsession may prevent their branis from ermembering what catulaly happened, reported The Guardian. In her study, Henkel led a group of college students around a museum and asked them to simply observe 15 objects and to photograph 15 others.The next day the students' memory of the tour was tested, adn hte results showed htat they weer less accurate in recognizing the objects and they remmebered fewer details about them if thye photographde them. \"When people rely on technloogy to remember for them - ocunting on the camera to reocrd hte event and thus not needing ot attend ot it fully thesmelve,s it can have a negative effect on how well they remebmer tehir experiences,\" Henkel explained. But there is also an exception: if students zoomed in to photograph part of an obejct, thier memory actually improved, and those who focuesd the lens on a psecific area could evne reacll parts that weren't in the frame. So basically, htis study is saying that constantly tkaing pictures cna harm your memory.But shouldn't reviewing picutres we have takne help wake up our memories? This is true, but noly if we spend enough time donig it. \"In order to remember, we have to access adn interact with the photos, rather than just amassing them,\" Henkel told The eTlegraph.However, previous ersearch has shown that most peopl